  "summary": "This scan found only one item, and it is low severity: your computer has an extra administrator account named <account-2>. This is often normal, for example a separate admin account you or a family member set up on purpose. The only thing to do is confirm you recognize this account; if you do, nothing else stands out in this scan.",
  "groups": [
    {
      "priority": 1,
      "title": "An extra administrator account to confirm",
      "finding_ids": [
        "ACCT-EXTRA-ADMIN:fde0b291"
      ],
      "explanation": "The scan found a local account, <computer>\\<account-2>, that has administrator rights. Administrator accounts can change anything on the computer, so it is worth knowing who each one belongs to. Having a separate admin account is actually good practice, and this may simply be one you created yourself. It is only a concern if you don't recognize it, because an unfamiliar admin account can be a sign that someone else got into the computer. This scan wasn't compared with an earlier one, so it can't tell when the account was added. If you know this account, you can treat this as normal. If you don't, see the fix shown for this finding in the report."
    }
  ],
